From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Sat, 24 Sep 1994 21:26:26 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-469] What Happens First?

Pete McClusky:
> Sufficient to convince the uploadee
> that there is no more change than when he goes to sleep? Sufficient to
> convince someone who accepts Mark Miller's credit theory of identity that
> enough information has been transfered to call the uploaded entity by the
> same name as the original person?

Sufficient for the credit theory along with those aspects of personality
important to a person.  For example most people desire sex, which requires 
extensive molecular simulation of the hormonal system.  Most people
desire most of their long term memories to remain intact.  An Alzheimer's 
patient can give credit to their childhood photograph, but that's not 
considered a sign of returned health.  An upload that lost its memories
like that might not survive very long, vs. either humans augmented
in other ways (smart drugs, brain expansion, nanochip implants, etc.)
or artificially constructed or evolved self-motivated software.

>  I am too impatient for the benefits of uploading to wait for microtubule
> or complete hormonal simulation.

Do we really know what the price of losing these capabilities is?  Are
you willing to make yourself a gunea pig, and risk obliteration of
the most basic parts of your personality to upload early?  Perhaps,
if Robin Hanson is right about the economic advantage of uploading
early, at some still quite lossy point this will in fact be the 
rational thing to do.  It's a very interesting and unresolved issue.

>  2 centuries at current rates? That sounds like about 2^100 or 10^33 times
> as much processing power; a number greater than the number of atoms in my
> body.

Indeed, it would take more than this power to properly simulate the
quantum mechanics of every atom in your body, which is a good first
approximation to simulating hormonal and synapse chemistry and
structure (a non negligable fraction of those atoms).  Hopefully we 
will find much higher level abstractions and simulation short-cuts: but
as I have said, this is an unsolved problem.  When projecting what 
will come before what, it's reasonable to take a conservative approach,
and not assume that we will solve problems of such an unknown, basic
nature, or at least not assume that the solutions will be easy.  If 
the predictions are wrong (any of these projections by anybody is 
likely wrong in many ways) we should be ready to switch gears and adapt 
to the new circumstances: even, perhaps, if that means uploading as monks.
I argued, in my essay "Uploading and Sexual Engineering", that it
may make evolutionary sense to upload as fetishists, so that sexual 
desires are turned into survival motivations for the new environement.  

I agree that the last thing we need is a "50 year plan" like 
the projections we get out of NASA, which are obsolete by the time they
get funded.  I can't say strongly enough that my projections are not
intended to become cast in stone plans: quite the opposite, I want
to explore a wide variety of options to enable flexible response.
Indeed, meta discussions about the methodology of prediction itself
are of great interest to me.

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

