From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Sat, 24 Sep 1994 23:16:47 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-470] Armed/Economic Revolution

>From: TORREY <LES_TWH@flo.org>
>To: anarchy-list@cwi.nl
>Subject: Economic Revolution
>
>...An armed insurrection cannot succeed against a large, powerful
>army with sophisiticated weapons.

"Anyone who claims that popular struggles are doomed to defeat by modern
military technology must find it literally incredible that France and the
United States suffered defeat in Vietnam; that the Shah no longer rules
Iran; Somosa in Nicaragua; that Portugal was expelled from Angola and
Mozambique; England from Palestine and Ireland; and France from Algeria."

	- Allan Goetlieb

"The hope of the future does not rest, as commonly believed, in winning the
peoples of the 'buffer fringe' to one superpower or the other, but rather in
the invention of new weapons and new tactics that will be so cheap to obtain
and so easy to use that they will increase the effectiveness of guerilla
warfare so greatly that the employment of our present weapons of mass
destruction will become futile and, on this basis, there can be a revival of
democracy and of political decentralization in all three parts of the world."

	- Carroll Quigley, "The Evolution of Civilizations," p. 418

Also see William Marina's article "Weapons, Technology, and Legitimacy: the
Second Amendment in Global Perspective" in _Firearms_and_Violence_, edited
by Don B. Kates Jr.

>...By arming yourself you give your opponent 'permission' to use lethal force.
        
No more so than you do by being unarmed.  The only difference is that if you're
unarmed then you're relatively defenseless.  History is replete with examples
of conquerors victorious over unarmed peoples, e.g., the US conquest of the
Philippine Republic earlier this century (the Philippines gave up their arms
in their peace treaty with Spain just previously - and it still took 70,000
Marines to conquer them).  And while being armed is no guarantee of freedom, to
paraphrase Thomas Jefferson: "A country that wants to be unarmed and free,
wants what never has been and never will be."

Arguing the impossibility of the armed resistance strategy is false and beside
the point.  History clearly shows that it _can_ work.  That doesn't mean that
it is the only good strategy, that it is the best one, or that it can work on
its own without the help of additional complementary strategies.  What you
call an "economic revolution" has been the necessary precursor of all success-
ful political revolutions in history.  When the productive class has been on
the side of the victorious revolutionaries thoughout history, the result has
been a freer society.  When the productive class has not been on their side,
military victory has only brought on tyranny.  Examples of the former include
the Persian Wars, the Swiss, Dutch, and Americans Wars of Independence, and
probably others I'm unaware of.  You've done a better job of listing examples
of the latter kind of "revolution" (which is actually a putch or coup d'etat)
than I could have.

By all means, proceed with the "economic revolution," a.k.a., the counter-
economy (Samuel Edward Konkin III), the "informal sector" (Hernando DeSoto/
Enrique Ghersi), the "underground economy," etc.  And by all means avoid
centralized militias; if guerilla warfare ever becomes necessary then command
must be decentralized for tactical purposes anyways, as Mao pointed out.
Stick to civil disobedience as long as possible.

But don't pretend that it'll never come down to armed conflict, or that
nothing good can ever come of that.  It may, and it can.  Hopefully, it won't.

Tim Starr - Renaissance Now!  Think Universally, Act Selfishly

